I'm Mike Massimino I'm a senior advisor  for space programs here at the intrepid  museum I'm also a engineering professor  at Columbia University and a former  astronaut the two missions that I were  on both were shuttle missions to service  Hubble Space Telescope first one was  sts-109 in 2002 and my second mission  was the final Hubble servicing mission  in 2009 sts-125 one of the defining  characteristics of the telescope is that  it was built and designed to be serviced  by astronauts and by the Space Shuttle  you know the size of the telescope was  dictated by the size of the payload Bay  you know in the the altitude of it was  dictated by the altitude that the help  that the shuttle kid could reach as an  astronaut it's a it's a it's a great  mission because you I had a sense that I  was contributing to something that was  really worthwhile my favorite images  were always the early release images the  ones that came down first when we worked  did our work on the telescope you know  we did as best we could but they really  can't take any observations for a while  and so for about a month there you don't  know for sure if what you did work he  thinks so but I go let's see what the  thing looks like so for my first flight  the Cole nebula is my favorite because I  was outside to install the ACS and  hooked it up and this beautiful image  the cone nebula which is kind of like a  star nursery and made like the front  page of the papers and stuff and that's  pretty cool you know again it's very  beautiful may not be the most beautiful  one may not be the most important  scientifically but it's my favorite  because it showed that the instrument  work my favorite one from my second  mission is just a dot of light  is the Space Telescope imaging  spectrograph first light it worked it  picked up a star and the thing powered  back up and the repair was correct and  worked and that that little image of  this is like faint star there's not one  of my favorite images it looks like a  light bulb  I guess we all kind of have this a you  know affection for the for the telescope  and what it does and it respect further  for the science that comes out of it but  also the engineering wonder that it is  it's a it's an amazing machine I think  it's a greatest scientific instrument  ever built
 

